WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.189 --> 00:00:03.169
Did you figure out what the problem was?

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:07.379
Let’s trace through this code and see where
the list went awry.

00:00:07.379 --> 00:00:10.510
The first three lines of the code are acceptable.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.710
But think of what happens on line 4.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:20.650
When we remove Juneau, every list item to
the right of Juneau moves left one position.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.420
I will type a comment on the right to simulate
what is happening here.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:37.030
When Juneau is removed, the list becomes Montgomery,
Phoenix, LittleRock with no space, and Sacramento.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:40.289
What position is Little Rock in now?

00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:41.289
Position 2.

00:00:41.289 --> 00:00:45.110
Thus, we need to make one of two changes to
fix this code.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:50.620
Either we need to change the position on line
6 to position 2, or move the line of code

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.550
in front of line 4 so that Little Rock is
changed first and then Juneau is removed.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:01.190
I will do the first change since it involves
less change.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.900
You may have times when you need to remove
an item from a list, but save the value that

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.330
has been removed and use it in code.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:14.090
For example, someone may remove a tool from
a toolbox, and you may want to display the

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.729
name of the tool that has been removed.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:21.270
The pop method removes the last item of a
list but allows one to use it in code just

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.790
after removing it.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:35.970
I will add a line in front of the print statement
on line 7 and type 

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.920
print(capitals.pop()).

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.869
This statement will remove Sacramento from
the list and display it.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:49.100
I will rerun this code, and we will see the
popped item and the remaining items in the

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.020
list, the remaining three state capitals.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.040
You can specify an index number to pop a specific
item.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.890
For example, if I add a 0 between the parentheses,
the first item on the list, Montgomery, will

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.430
be popped.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:11.640
I will rerun the code, and we see the popped
city and then the remaining cities in the

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.750
list.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:19.519
Sometimes, you want to sort a list or reverse
the order in which a list is displayed.

00:02:19.519 --> 00:02:22.489
Reversing a list does not sort it in descending
order.

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:24.880
Reverse and sort are two different methods.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:32.470
I will add a reverse method above the print
statement on line 7 to demonstrate this difference.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.880
The statement looks like this: capitals.reverse().

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:44.209
Then, I will rerun the code, and the list
of cities prints in the reverse order in which

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:46.599
the list was entered and then manipulated.

